{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-10735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $y=-\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-10735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}}=-3{x}^{-7}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{-7}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-7}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-7){x}^{-7-1} \\\\  & =21{x}^{-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{{x}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-10735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-10735" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-10735" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{21}{{x}^{8}}"] ["math" "18{x}^{5}"] ["math" "21{x}^{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{{x}^{-8}}"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{{x}^{8}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-10735" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{21}{{x}^{8}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-124410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$3!-0!=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-124410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given expression is a subtraction of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $3!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n3!=3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3!-0! & =6-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-124410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-124410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-124410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"] ["text" "Undefined"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-124410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-104040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-104040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a break exactly at $x=-3$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-104040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-104040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-104040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/701e652c0db6997cae3fe82828cfe37a7dd4ddb07712fb0dd2b747f47b94a3d0.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/9eb01437ae94e15e061548f8be50dfd101d5350fb3550a131afe7ff5fd975c10.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/a3026fef480f96f0b1d1583c0136b3b7c82054bbce13b2480a14fa37cba608f6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/deacb7f7b36ae12e236a3d5eae46def61ba2570a2cd8d7a4bb4527ce9d2cec7b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/e56e3c7e64c1bfa06b48a00225f3b7cf0551397ab96d160c88989ea24b70584b.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-104040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/deacb7f7b36ae12e236a3d5eae46def61ba2570a2cd8d7a4bb4527ce9d2cec7b.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-39135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+7x+3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-39135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 8 & 7 & 3 \\\\  &  & -6 & -6 & -3 \\\\  & 2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+3}=2{x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+3)(2{x}^{2}+2x+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(1)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)^{3}+8(0)^{2}+7(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-39135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-39135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-39135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/585b326719cd74e9a7b26892df49742083ccafeea83379a517552f78be83e5ee.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/851b996ce1dab2e5777b5c7475785863009c0b8519b2805da72cbb4c07afcb47.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/98705a867d25e4f8991c29e9cda964b694c678cb1cb2de638f4d1d2f1ae37200.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/a11984f9a9de7e220103525a3c925dee83a9eed37c4901f46046de7e80bc3a7f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-39135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the antiderivative of \n$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}+{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}+{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{(3+1)}-\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/3}}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{(-1)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-113848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+C"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}+C"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{x}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+4{x}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}+C"] ["math" "\\sqrt[3]{x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{x}{4}+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-262221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The distance between the points $A(3,1,2)$ and $B(5,-3,4)$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-262221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,1,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(5,-3,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(5-3)^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-262221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-262221" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-262221" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-41579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify $\\frac{5+b}{3b}+\\frac{6-b}{3b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-41579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a common denominator of $3b,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5+b}{3b}+\\frac{6-b}{3b} & = \\\\ \\frac{5+b+6-b}{3b} & = \\\\ \\frac{(5+6)+(b-b)}{3b} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{3b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-41579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-41579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-41579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11+2b}{3b}"] ["math" "\\frac{11-2b}{3b}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{3b}"] ["math" "\\frac{2b+1}{3b}"] ["math" "\\frac{2b-1}{3b}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-41579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{3b}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the solutions of the equation $(x-1)^{2}=-64?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =-64 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-64} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 8\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=1+8\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=1-8\\,\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-113253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-1\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=1\\pm 64\\,\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=1\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=8\\pm 16\\,\\text{i}"] ["math" "x=8\\pm \\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-113253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=1\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-22978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which angle is coterminal with $-{175}^{∘}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-22978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in degrees), we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{175}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{175}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={185}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{175}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={545}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than ${545}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{175}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{175}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{535}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{175}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}=-{895}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-{895}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is ${545}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-22978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-22978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-22978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{505}^{∘}"] ["math" "-{800}^{∘}"] ["math" "{180}^{∘}"] ["math" "{450}^{∘}"] ["math" "{545}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-22978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{545}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-148444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}$ is defined for $x>-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find ${f}^{-1}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-148444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{(2y+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{(2y+1)^{2}} \\\\ (2y+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{(2y+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x}} \\\\ |2y+1| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 2y+1 & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y>-\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n|2y+1|=2y+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-148444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-148444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-148444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+1"] ["math" "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-1"] ["math" "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-2"] ["math" "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-148444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-86322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡{135}^{∘}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-86322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${135}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡{135}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-86322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5e83ddaf40aff0a0e544b7929d30265c2da479df80e04d5aff35715ae9400857"} {"question" "q-86322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-86322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"} {"question" "q-86322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13975009/state.json" "file_sha256" "a15d56d3e9e46bdfef3b65a6a292c95fb40129389b2ae7a405d58b179b82e446"}] "usage" {"q-41579" [] "q-39135" []} "basis" 801 "retractions" [[17592186069252 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{5 + b}{3b} + \\frac{6 - b}{3b}$."] [17592186129652 :question/problem "Given that $(x + 3)$ is a factor of $f(x) = 2x^{3} + 8x^{2} + 7x + 3$, which of the following curves is generated by $y = f(x)$?\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186067044 17592186067045 17592186067046 17592186067047 17592186067048 17592186127707 17592186127708 17592186127709 17592186127710 17592186127711] "immutable_fields" [[17592186067043 :answer-field/correct] [17592186067043 :answer-field/id] [17592186067043 :answer-field/key] [17592186067043 :answer-field/type] [17592186127706 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127706 :answer-field/id] [17592186127706 :answer-field/key] [17592186127706 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
